(*
Issue #300/#550: parseSimpleExpr's addop loop ('a + a + a + ...') folds a
flat chain of same-precedence operators ITERATIVELY -- one 'while' loop
iteration per '+' -- rather than recursing once per operator the way '('
does through parseFactor. ExprDepth/MaxExprDepth (see the comment on
ExprDepth, Parser.h) originally only bounded the genuinely recursive
re-entries into expression parsing, so this loop never bumped it at all:
nothing capped how many BinaryExpr levels one long flat chain could stack
up, independent of how deep any enclosing '(' nesting already was. An
adversarial chain built this way used to parse to completion regardless of
length, then overflow the real stack later on ~any consumer that recurses
over the resulting tree -- including the tree's own destructor once it is
torn down (issue #551), which is a fix too invasive for this same change
(see that issue).

1000 terms is well past the 500-deep ceiling (MaxExprDepth, ParseExpr.cpp)
while staying fast to parse here; see
test-lit/Driver/ParserRobustness/ for an end-to-end version at the real
crash threshold. Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time,
matching deeply-nested-parens-report-one-diagnostic-not-a-crash.pas's own
precedent -- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is
deterministic; nobody should have to look at 1000 terms to read this file,
so the lit directives come first.

The depth-limit diagnostic fires once and stops folding rather than
building past the ceiling; what is left is the ordinary, bounded fallout
of one expression failing to consume its remaining, un-folded input, the
same shape as that file's own trailing diagnostics.

RUN: not %plang_ir -dump-parse-tree %s 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
RUN: grep -c "error:" %t.err | FileCheck --check-prefix=COUNT --strict-whitespace --match-full-lines %s
*)

